  "diagnosis_text": "The h1\u2192h5 path is up, but it is experiencing severe queueing rather than a link-down fault:\n\n`h1 \u2014 s4 \u2014 s2 \u2014 s1 \u2014 s3 \u2014 s6 \u2014 h5`\n\n**Evidence**\n- `ping h1\u2192h5`: 0% loss, but RTT was extremely high and variable: **64\u2013390 ms**, **212 ms average**.\n- A 5-second TCP test from h1 to h5 achieved only **~0.21 Mbit/s at the sender**; the receiver summary reported **0 received bytes**, so end-to-end TCP delivery is severely impaired in that measurement.\n- The same symptom is not unique to h1: `h2\u2192h5` (which joins the same path at s4) had **83\u2013420 ms RTT**, **241 ms average**.\n- The standout path interface is **s2 port 1, toward s1**. In a 2-second sample it showed:\n  - **~1.0 Mbit/s transmit rate**\n  - **37.9 KB / 25 packets queued**\n  - **352 queue-overlimit events**\n- Other sampled onward interfaces (`s1\u2192s3`, `s3\u2192s6`, and `s6\u2192h5`) had no backlog, overlimits, drops, or errors. All topology links are active.\n\n**Diagnosis**\nThe likely bottleneck is the **egress queue/rate limit or congestion on s2 port 1 (s2\u2192s1)**. It is a shared segment for both h1\u2192h5 and h2\u2192h5, which explains the high latency for both. The measured queue overlimits indicate that traffic is arriving faster than this interface can service it.\n\nThere were no measured physical-port errors or ONOS-reported link failures. The counters do not identify what traffic is filling the queue, so I cannot distinguish a configured low rate from competing traffic using the available data. The next operator action is to inspect the traffic/shaping policy and competing flows on **s2 port 1 / the s2\u2192s1 link**.",
